<description>
Turn black dents in two colored regions into marked opposite-color patches.

Input structure:
- Black background with two large foreground colors.
- The large regions contain small black dents and a few one-cell color specks
  or protrusions along region boundaries.

Rule:
- Locally clean the two foreground regions: isolated specks become black, and
  one-cell protrusions surrounded by the other region take that region's color.
- Each original black dent whose cleaned 3x3 neighborhood is mostly one
  foreground color becomes the center of a 3x3 patch in the opposite color.
- The dent center itself is recolored teal (8).

Key insight: The teal pixels mark the original black defects; the surrounding
3x3 patch uses the other foreground color, after first removing boundary noise.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "black dents inside two large foreground-color regions, with possible one-cell foreground specks or boundary protrusions",
  "transform": "first locally clean the foreground regions by removing isolated specks and recoloring one-cell protrusions to the surrounding region color; then each original black dent whose cleaned 3x3 neighborhood is mostly one foreground color becomes a 3x3 patch of the other foreground color with the dent center recolored teal"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 20-30 rows x 20-30 cols (square)
  colors: 2 foreground colors (any non-black, non-teal) on black background
  regions:
    layout: 2-3 axis-aligned rectangles of 2 fg colors, painted in order (later covers earlier = simple occlusion); together covering 68-76% of grid
    each_rectangle: 100-400 cells; partially overlapping with at least one other rectangle
    visible_shape: each color's visible area appears L-shaped or rectangular due to occlusion, not by staircase splitting
    background: fills corners/edges not covered by any rectangle; ~24-31% of grid
  dents:
    count: 12-22 clusters (scales with grid area), split roughly equally between the two regions
    shape: mostly 1-cell; ~10-15% are 2-cell (orthogonally adjacent pair)
    placement: each dent center's clean 3x3 neighborhood must contain >=5 host-color cells; 8-35% of dents are on the region boundary (4-neighbor includes non-host or grid edge)
    role: black (0) holes punched into the clean foreground regions
  noise:
    count: 7-22 isolated 1-cell wrong-color specks
    in_bg: foreground-color pixels scattered in background area (majority of noise)
    at_boundary: 0-7 cells of one fg color placed inside the other region, within 1 cell of the region-region boundary
  output_rule: |
    1. Clean regions: remove noise specks (isolated fg in bg -> black; boundary contamination -> correct region color)
    2. For each black dent whose cleaned 3x3 neighborhood is mostly one fg color (>=5 host cells):
       fill its 3x3 neighborhood with the OTHER fg color, then mark the dent center as teal (8)
    3. 3x3 patches clip to grid bounds and may overflow into bg or the other region at edges

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid size N in [20,30] and two distinct fg colors (not black/teal).
    2. Build clean regions: paint 2-3 axis-aligned rectangles in order (later covers earlier). Use 2 fg colors; when 3 rects, two share a color. Ensure partial overlap between adjacent rects and 68-76% total coverage.
    3. Place dent centers: 12-22 total, ~equal per color. Center must have >=5 host cells in 3x3. Allow placement on region boundary (8-35%). ~10-15% are 2-cell adjacent pairs.
    4. Build output - stamp each dent's 3x3 neighborhood with the opposite fg color (clipping at grid bounds), then set dent centers to teal (8).
    5. Build input from clean regions - set dent center cells to black (0). Add 7-22 noise specks: place ~70% as isolated fg-color pixels in bg area (within 1-3 cells of a region edge), and ~30% as cross-color contamination at the region-region boundary.
```
</generation_spec>
